  <h2>What a portfolio is really for</h2>
  <p>An architecture portfolio is not a scrapbook of everything you have ever drawn. It is a curated argument about the kind of designer you are. Whether it is going to a tutor, a Part 1 employer, or a masters admissions panel, the reader is asking one question: can this person think spatially, resolve a design, and communicate it clearly? Every page should answer yes. The fastest way to weaken a portfolio is to include work that does not — a mediocre project drags down the strong ones around it.</p>

  <h2>What to include</h2>
  <p>Lead with three to five complete projects. For each, show the full arc: the site and brief, your concept or parti, the developed design through plans, sections and elevations, and at least one resolved moment — a detail, a model photo, a considered render. Reviewers want evidence of a process, not just a glossy final image. Include hand sketches, physical models, and iteration; these prove the thinking is yours and not lifted from software presets. A technical page or two (a construction detail, an environmental diagram) reassures employers you can build as well as dream.</p>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Put your single strongest project first and your second strongest last. Readers remember the opening and the closing pages most.</div>

  <h2>Curation is the whole game</h2>
  <p>Ruthless editing beats volume every time. A tight portfolio of four excellent projects will always outperform ten uneven ones. If you are unsure about a piece, cut it. Vary the work you keep so the collection shows range — a housing scheme, a small pavilion, an adaptive-reuse project and a technical study read very differently from four versions of the same studio brief. Tailor the selection to the reader: an environmentally focused practice wants to see your sustainability thinking near the front.</p>

  <h2>Layout and typography</h2>
  <p>Choose a grid and hold it across every page. Consistent margins, a single restrained typeface in two or three sizes, and generous white space do more for perceived quality than any amount of decoration. Let the drawings breathe — a strong image on a clean page reads as confidence. Number your pages, keep a quiet running header with the project name, and make sure every drawing carries a scale and a north point where relevant. Avoid dark, busy backgrounds and clashing accent colours; they date quickly and fight the work.</p>

  <h2>Page count and format</h2>
  <p>For a Part 1 application, aim for roughly 20 to 40 pages — enough to show depth, short enough to hold attention. Many practices also ask for a one or two page sample to sit alongside your CV, so keep a condensed version ready. Landscape A3 or A4 suits most spreads because it matches how drawings are composed, but pick the format that frames your work best and commit to it throughout.</p>

  <h2>Digital versus print</h2>
  <p>Most first contact is now digital, so export a single PDF under about 10 MB that opens fast and looks correct on screen. Design in RGB for screen but keep a print-ready version in CMYK if a studio asks for a physical copy at interview — a well-printed book you can talk through is still a powerful thing to put on the table. Name the file clearly with your name, never "portfolio_final_v7", and check every hyperlink and page order before you send.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
  <p>The usual failures are predictable: too many projects, no clear process, tiny unreadable text, drawings without scales, and renders that hide a design rather than explain it. Do not fill space for the sake of it, do not include group work without stating your exact role, and never send a portfolio you have not proofread. Ask a tutor or friend to read it cold and tell you which project they remember — if it is not the one you are proudest of, re-order it.</p>
  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Watch out.</strong> Always credit collaborators and clearly mark which parts of group projects were your own work. Ambiguity here reads as dishonesty.</div>

